 "title": "Battle of Cannae",
 "date_label": "2 August 216 BC",
 "year": -216,
 "intro": "By the summer of 216 BC Hannibal had spent two years loose in Italy and had already destroyed Roman armies at the Trebia and Lake Trasimene. Rome answered by sending both consuls of the year, Lucius Aemilius Paullus and Gaius Terentius Varro, with eight legions and as many allied troops, the largest army the Republic had ever put into a single field. Hannibal drew it south into Apulia by seizing the hilltop depot at Cannae, and the two armies camped facing each other along the river Aufidus, the modern Ofanto, a few kilometres from the Adriatic. The consuls took command on alternate days. On 2 August it was Varro's turn: he crossed the river and offered battle on the narrow plain below the Cannae ridge, trusting the sheer weight of his infantry. Hannibal, outnumbered by almost two to one, arranged his army so that this weight would carry the Romans into a trap.",
 "outcome": "A crushing Carthaginian victory: the Roman army was surrounded and largely destroyed, Paullus was killed and Varro fled, in one of the bloodiest single days of battle in ancient history.",
 "uncertainty": "Where the battle was fought is disputed. This map follows the view of most modern historians (among them Goldsworthy) and of the Cannae site museum: the right, south-eastern bank, in the plain between the river and the Cannae ridge, with the Romans facing roughly south-west and their right on the river. Older scholars such as Delbrück put it on the left bank. The river is drawn on its modern course, but the Aufidus has moved since 216 BC and probably ran a few hundred metres further north-west, which would have widened the field; the frontages here are therefore slightly compressed; the river is drawn dashed for that reason. The camp sites, the crossings and Varro's route are conjectural. The legions are drawn in the usual three lines, though Connolly thought the triarii stayed behind to guard the camps. Livy's wind, the Volturnus, is usually read as a south-easterly; Dodge made it easterly. Numbers follow Polybius and Goldsworthy; Livy's differ, and modern estimates of the Roman dead vary widely.",
 "alt_sites": [
