   "id": "camps",
   "title": "Camps on the Aufidus",
   "time": "1 August",
   "narrative": "The Romans had shadowed Hannibal south and built two camps astride the Aufidus: the main one, holding about two-thirds of the army, on the north-western bank, and a smaller one across the river some two kilometres away to cover foraging on that side. Hannibal pitched his camp on the same bank as the main Roman camp. On 1 August he drew up his army and offered battle, but Paullus, in command that day, would not be hurried. Hannibal then sent his Numidian horsemen across the river to fall on the water parties of the smaller camp.",
