   "id": "varro-crosses",
   "title": "Varro crosses the river",
   "time": "2 August, dawn",
   "narrative": "Command passed to Varro, who meant to fight. At first light the army marched out of the main camp, forded the river and joined the troops of the smaller camp on the south-eastern bank. He formed it facing roughly south-west across a cramped plain: the citizen cavalry under Paullus on the right against the river, the allied cavalry under Varro himself on the left beneath the Cannae ridge, and between them the heavy infantry under Gnaeus Servilius Geminus. The maniples were closed up and stacked many times deeper than their width, so some 55,000 men held only about a kilometre of front, screened by the light-armed velites. About 10,000 men stayed behind to hold the camps.",
