  {
   "id": "crescent",
   "title": "Hannibal forms the crescent",
   "time": "Early morning",
   "narrative": "Hannibal answered by sending his Balearic slingers and light spearmen over the river as a screen and following with the rest of the army at two crossings. Beside the river he massed the Spanish and Gallic heavy horse under Hasdrubal, opposite the Roman cavalry; on the far right, under the ridge, stood the Numidians. The centre was Gallic and Spanish foot in alternating companies, which he pushed forward into a thin convex crescent, while the veteran African infantry waited in deep blocks on either side of it, held back from the bulge. Hannibal and his brother Mago took post in the centre. Livy says a wind the locals called the Volturnus rose against the Romans and drove dust into their faces, while the morning sun fell side-on to both armies.",
