   "id": "slaughter",
   "title": "The slaughter",
   "time": "Afternoon to evening",
   "narrative": "For hours the trapped army was cut down from the edges inward. Livy tells how Paullus, wounded and sitting on a stone, refused a horse that would have let him escape and died with his men; Servilius Geminus and Marcus Minucius Rufus fell too, with dozens of senators and military tribunes. Polybius puts the Roman dead at about 70,000 and Livy at about 48,000, against 5,700 to 8,000 on Hannibal's side, most of them Gauls of the centre. Only a few thousand Romans cut their way out and scattered to the camps and nearby towns.",
