   "title": "The camps surrender",
   "time": "Night and 3 August",
   "narrative": "The men left to guard the main camp had attacked Hannibal's camp during the battle, Polybius says, and were driven back once the fighting on the plain was over. That night a group from the smaller camp under the tribune Publius Sempronius Tuditanus cut its way through to the main camp, and from there several thousand survivors slipped away to Canusium. The next day both camps surrendered. Within weeks Capua and much of southern Italy went over to Hannibal, but Rome refused to treat with him and fought on.",
